  "name": "Nt- ProBNP (N-Terminal Pro B Type Natriuretic Peptide) ",
  "code": "NT PROBNP",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<table><thead><tr><th><div>NT-proBNP Level (pg/mL)</div></th><th><div>Interpretation</div></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><div>Less than 300 pg/mL</div></td><td><div>Normal NT-proBNP level in individuals under 75 years old. It indicates no significant heart-related issues.</div></td></tr><tr><td><div>300 - 450 pg/mL</div></td><td><div>Slightly elevated NT-proBNP level in individuals under 75 years old. It may suggest mild heart strain or early heart failure. Further evaluation is required to determine the cause.</div></td></tr><tr><td><div>450 - 900 pg/mL</div></td><td><div>Moderately elevated NT-proBNP level in individuals under 75 years old. It may indicate moderate heart failure or other heart-related conditions. Additional testing and assessment are necessary.</div></td></tr><tr><td><div>More than 900 pg/mL</div></td><td><div>Markedly elevated NT-proBNP level in individuals under 75 years old. It suggests severe heart failure or significant heart-related issues. Immediate medical attention and further evaluation are essential.</div></td></tr><tr><td><div>Age-adjusted Reference Ranges</div></td><td><div>For individuals aged 75 years and older, higher NT-proBNP levels may be considered normal due to age-related changes in the heart. Age-adjusted reference ranges may vary by laboratory and need to be considered in the interpretation.</div></td></tr></tbody></table>",
